  unit: { count: 321, covers: "Every rule at the level where it lives. THE PROOF AND THE GATE (W2): isCaptured, isDecryptedHttps and latestFlowTimes read source captured only, so an imported or sent row proves nothing; the machine records since on not-detected and re-invoke; useFlows counts the listings the core answered. THE SESSION (W3): the machine's chosen, another and opensOn (another device never opens on this machine), everProven for the dot, sessionTraffic keeping only captured rows and tunnels at or after since, and the useFlows setup scope listing unfiltered. THE READINGS (W4): the verdicts that read a probe beside them - the not-decrypting remedy defers to a trusted certificate, the end credits this machine only when both probes read done, the reversal names only what is installed. THE HOSTS (W5): platformChoices per host OS (the iOS Simulator on macOS only), canProbe, the shell's three-way proxy reading with unknown where it cannot read on macOS, Windows and a host with no reader, and quit reading unknown as none. THE FILES (W7): the CA's DER and Apple profile derived from its PEM with stable UUIDs, reach's ca_urls per format over IPv4 and IPv6, and a sweep over 8 platforms x 4 hosts x armed or not that every certificate task names one file, the one its URL and save control produce, with the iPhone's separate trust step and each platform's not-decrypting check. THE RECORDING (W1): the shell's scenario feature, the harness's API front, and the walkthrough command's judge (a finding no chapter names is refused before any build) and assembler." }
  integration: { count: 273, covers: "Each WU's contract through the real thing. tests/api.rs and tests/reach.rs (W7): /ca.crt, /ca.pem, /ca.cer and /ca.mobileconfig each answer with their type, a file name matching the URL and the CA's bytes, a non-certificate PEM fails the two derived routes, and an armed core's /reach names the three URLs, each answering there. App.test.tsx: the launch paints no setup instruction while the read is in flight (R1, the launch flash waived at e2e and pinned here on the synchronous render), a core that does not answer gets the statement with no steps, the launch read's patience, the race orders review rounds 1-3 found, a banner only for a captured row after since, re-entry and the dot keeping connected (R4-R7). Onboarding.test.tsx: the four probe readings on macOS and Windows (R12, waived at e2e because a hermetic run cannot set the real trust store and proxy), a failed action opening its route, the Simulator on macOS only, the probe row where a probe answers, the network task naming its address and interface and the no-address remedy, a platform switch remounting its tasks, each platform saving and naming its own file. onboardingMatrix.test.tsx: 12913 states - every host x offered platform x reach x probe pair x traffic history, plus every offered action's outcome - judged against a model written apart from the screen. xtask/tests/walkthrough.rs: a reel whose anchor never painted turns the walkthrough red and writes no video for it." }
  e2e: { count: 12, covers: "THE EPIC'S FULL FLOW, through the real Tauri window against a real ephemeral core. cargo xtask ui e2e green at W6: 4 of 4 spec files, 46 cases, the onboarding spec's 11 among them. The plan's three journeys, no waiver: a core holding only a sent row launches into setup with the try task waiting and no row, and the revealed list later shows the row marked sent; set up another device keeps the dot connected with no task and no success line, then the Android emulator's guide is open and waiting, then a new captured request gives capturing and the success line; filtered to no-such-path.test, the dot reopens on the last platform chosen, a request captures under the filter, and back keeps the filter. The walkthrough spec re-run at W6 for the after recording: 24 reels, 153 chapters, every chapter's expected element present before its frame, beside W1's before. Waived at e2e and pinned below it as the plan's test table names: the launch flash (R1), the per-half layout (R12), and a real phone's installer (W7), whose evidence is the owner's installs on a real iPhone and a real Android phone." }
